#
# OPERATOR REPOSITION, not a kidnap. The operator moves the body deliberately
# to set up the next run, and the robot is told where it went WHERE THE
# PLATFORM ALLOWS IT. That is the whole difference from Kidnap.srv, which is
# an unannounced teleport whose point is that the estimator must recover.
#
# Served as /faults/reposition by ral_faults/fault_manager.py. Injection
# point: /world/<world>/set_pose (gz entity pose) - the same gz boundary the
# kidnap uses, because moving a body in gz has exactly one boundary.
#
# `new_pose` is in the gz WORLD frame, like every other gz entity pose, and is
# NOT converted through world_to_map.
#
# `new_pose.position.z` is IGNORED and replaced by the manager's kidnap_z
# (the robot's spawn height from its profile), for the same reason Kidnap.srv
# states: the pose carries x/y/yaw only and z = 0 drops the wheels through the
# floor. Linear and angular velocity are NOT zeroed either, so the body keeps
# the twist it had.
#
# THREE THINGS SEPARATE THIS FROM A KIDNAP, and the manager reports all three:
#
#   1. The destination is PRE-FLIGHTED against the committed map with the
#      robot's own footprint. A destination inside a wall is refused and the
#      body does not move. `kidnap` is deliberately unchecked - teleporting
#      into geometry is a legitimate thing to inflict on a localizer.
#   2. The estimator is reseeded only ON A PLATFORM THAT EXPOSES A SEED. For
#      a velocity_twist platform (the Roomba, running AMCL) the manager
#      publishes /initialpose. ArduPilot's EKF3 has no equivalent, so on the
#      car NOTHING is reseeded and the estimate diverges by the teleport
#      distance until GPS pulls it back.
#   3. The `activated` detail states `estimate_reseeded: true|false` with the
#      reason, so the timeline shows whether the robot's belief moved with
#      its body.
#
# `fault_id` works exactly as in Kidnap.srv: the join key for this instance,
# empty meaning "allocate one".

string fault_id
geometry_msgs/Pose new_pose
---
string fault_id
bool success
string message
